 ],
 "translation": "The others crowded round the victorious Hannibal with congratulations and urged him, now that he had come through so great a fight, to take the rest of the day and the following night for rest himself and to give it to his tired soldiers. But Maharbal, the cavalry commander, who thought there must be no delay at all, said: \"No: so that you may know what this battle has achieved, on the fifth day you will dine as victor on the Capitol. Follow me; I will go ahead with the cavalry, so that they learn we have arrived before they learn we are coming.\" To Hannibal the idea seemed too happy, and too large, for him to grasp at once. So he said he praised Maharbal's zeal, but that he needed time to weigh the plan. Then Maharbal said: \"Evidently the gods have not given everything to the same man. You know how to win, Hannibal; how to use a victory, you do not.\" That day's delay is fairly widely believed to have saved the city and the empire.",
 "translation_note": "The first sentence is one long period: cum ... grātulārentur suādērentque (while the others ...), then the main subject Maharbal ... inquit. diē quīntō counts inclusively in the Roman way, so \"on the fifth day\" may mean four days later. victōriā ūtī: ūtor takes the ablative. mora ... crēditur ... fuisse is a personal passive (\"the delay is believed to have been\"), and salūtī is a dative of purpose (\"for salvation\").",
 "essay": [
  [
   {
    "t": "At Cannae in Apulia, in August 216 BC, Hannibal destroyed a Roman army under the consuls Lucius Aemilius Paullus and Gaius Terentius Varro, in what military historians, Britannica says, regard as a classic double envelopment.",
    "src": [
     2
    ]
   },
   {
    "t": "Roman losses are reported as about 55,000 by Livy and 70,000 by Polybius; the Carthaginians lost about 6,000.",
    "src": [
     2
    ],
    "doubt": true
   }
  ],
  [
   {
    "t": "In Livy, Maharbal promises that Hannibal will dine on the Capitol on the fifth day; when Hannibal asks for time, he delivers the famous rebuke, and Livy adds that the day's delay is believed to have saved Rome.",
    "src": [
     1
    ]
   },
   {
    "t": "Plutarch tells the same story in his Life of Fabius but gives the rebuke to \"Barca\" rather than Maharbal, and admits it is \"not easy to say\" why Hannibal held back.",
    "src": [
     3
    ]
   },
   {
    "t": "The disagreement over the speaker is itself a reason for caution.",
    "src": [
     1,
     3
    ],
    "doubt": true
   }
  ],
  [
   {
    "t": "Klaus Zimmermann, in a 2019 article in De Rebus Antiquis, argues that the exchange lacks historicity and that Livy's verdict reflects Roman hindsight.",
    "src": [
     4
    ],
    "doubt": true
   },
   {
    "t": "He also argues that a march on Rome was not feasible: Cannae was some 400 km away, about two weeks for the main army, so surprise was impossible; Hannibal's strength was manoeuvre and cavalry, not siege warfare; and Rome was walled and could replace its losses.",
    "src": [
     4
    ]
   },
   {
    "t": "On his reading, Hannibal's aim was to break up Rome's alliances in Italy, not to destroy the city, and his later march on Rome in 211 BC was a diversion to relieve Capua.",
    "src": [
     4
    ],
    "doubt": true
   }
  ],
  [
   {
    "t": "Not everyone has judged Hannibal so kindly; the Roman tradition Livy reports clearly thought the delay a mistake, and Zimmermann notes that even Livy's own sources disagreed.",
    "src": [
     1,
     4
    ],
    "doubt": true
   },
   {
    "t": "Britannica records the rebuke as Livy's report and notes that Rome went back to avoiding pitched battle while Hannibal's strength was gradually worn down.",
    "src": [
     2
    ]
   },
   {
    "t": "The line survives because it is quotable: a clean verdict on a decision that historians still debate.",
    "src": [
     1,
     4
    ],
    "doubt": true
   }
  ]
 ],
